1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:36,270
I am the Time Chronicler guarding a cosmic library of five thousand years of human history and I have come today to tell you the wildest tale of our very first words.

2
00:00:36,270 --> 00:01:10,500
Late at night my old library whispers one strange question in the dark, if a newborn child never hears a single human word, which language would rise from his mouth on its own.

3
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:46,850
Daily you gossip with friends in chat or text on smartphones among seven thousand living languages yet few ever stop to think where those very first words came from.

4
00:01:46,850 --> 00:02:22,880
One note is that we present only conventional research from ancient manuscripts fossils and forty dig sites worldwide which never opposes any religion faith or belief.

5
00:02:22,880 --> 00:03:02,110
But one hundred thousand years ago planet Earth held no books songs or spoken words and only silent stillness spread across misty primeval forests under starry skies.

6
00:03:02,110 --> 00:03:42,260
So think how that vast cosmic silence finally broke at dawn when shivering hairless apes opened their mouths and released the very first magical word of human history.

7
00:03:42,260 --> 00:04:18,530
In dry African savanna a vervet monkey gives a sharp screech for an eagle and a heavy grunt for a lurking leopard which quickly warns the whole troop about danger type.

8
00:04:18,530 --> 00:04:52,160
But no monkey in history has ever gossiped that the leopard seen yesterday across the northern river looked handsome because they lack any power to narrate past events.

9
00:04:52,160 --> 00:05:29,070
In the first three years a baby brain rapidly builds millions of neural links each second, so every word heard in this window becomes the base of future sentence skill.

10
00:05:29,070 --> 00:06:05,700
Animal calls signal only present danger since beasts cannot recount past hunts or dream of tomorrow while humans can gossip narrate and lie which starts the real mystery.

11
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:41,050
Two hundred thousand years ago in African savanna two hunters tracking game fell into deep trouble after spotting a giant woolly mammoth hiding behind yellow bushes.

12
00:06:41,050 --> 00:07:18,080
Afraid the prey would flee he could not shout aloud so he waved arms wildly like Charlie Chaplin in mad silent mime while his buddy wrongly thought he was swatting flies.

13
00:07:18,080 --> 00:07:56,950
Such comic confusion could bring starvation or crushing death under mammoth feet so primitive hunters urgently needed precise meaningful words beyond vague gestures.

14
00:07:56,950 --> 00:08:38,460
At six months a baby babble is a global sound test of all human tones, and warm replies from parents slowly turn that soft playful melody into full grammar bound speech.

15
00:08:38,460 --> 00:09:17,850
For five thousand years ancient societies from Egypt to Babylon firmly believed that humanity began with only one single mother tongue uniting every tribe across planet Earth.

16
00:09:17,850 --> 00:09:53,240
The old Babel myth says arrogant builders raised a colossal brick tower toward heaven so God scrambled their tongues into mutual gibberish and split all speech in Babylon.

17
00:09:53,240 --> 00:10:31,910
But keeping myths aside think what real science says about forty ancient sites and whether speech sprang from one single global Proto World source or many separate sparks.

18
00:10:31,910 --> 00:11:09,420
By the mid nineteenth century debates over the birth of speech in Paris conference halls turned so vicious that polite scholarly talks nearly collapsed into fistfights.

19
00:11:09,420 --> 00:11:51,170
Finally in eighteen sixty six the prestigious Linguistic Society of Paris passed a famous historic decree under Article Two banning all study of the origin of speech.

20
00:11:51,170 --> 00:12:29,240
That famous Article Two declared all papers talks and debates on the origin of language fully banned because scholars held no solid proof but only wild fantasy tales.

21
00:12:29,240 --> 00:13:03,790
German philologist Max Muller openly mocked five popular nineteenth century theories by giving them funny nicknames which widely exposed the absurdity of wild speculation.

22
00:13:03,790 --> 00:13:40,100
First came the Bow Wow Theory which claimed primitive humans coined the first words in ancient forests by copying barking dogs cuckoos and other loud sounds of wild nature.

23
00:13:40,100 --> 00:14:16,330
Second came the Pooh Pooh Theory which argued language began from cries of pain and disgust like yelling Ouch after stubbing a toe or saying Eww near rotting garbage.

24
00:14:16,330 --> 00:14:54,880
For two silent years I herded goats through desert storms without one word, feeding babies milk in silence while wondering what sound would leave their mouths first.

25
00:14:54,880 --> 00:15:34,470
Third came the Ding Dong Theory which claimed every object in wild nature holds a mystic hidden resonance that human vocal cords in crowded caves echoed into spoken words.

26
00:15:34,470 --> 00:16:14,780
Fourth stood the realistic Yo He Ho Theory where four hunters hauling a colossal hairy mammoth or boulder chanted Heave Ho in rhythm to match their strength up steep hills.

27
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:50,090
After dragging that boulder to the rocky hilltop their united cheers and rhythmic grunts slowly shaped into coordinated social speech and tribal bonding say researchers.

28
00:16:50,090 --> 00:17:26,000
After two silent years the bekos cry from the toddlers felt like victory to the Pharaoh, yet scholars found it was no Egyptian word but a Phrygian term meaning bread.

29
00:17:26,000 --> 00:18:03,230
These early theories sound funny but they fail one basic test because a wild barking dog can make loud noise yet it can never invent complex grammar rules for sentences.

30
00:18:03,230 --> 00:18:38,860
Rhythmic grunts can help strong men drag a heavy rock together but they can never express a complex future plan like fishing across the northern river tomorrow morning.

31
00:18:38,860 --> 00:19:15,930
To discover the true mother tongue ancient power hungry kings isolated newborn babies from human speech for proof in a cruel test now remembered as the Forbidden Experiment.

32
00:19:15,930 --> 00:19:51,120
In the seventh century BC Pharaoh Psamtik the First of Egypt firmly believed that his own Egyptians were the oldest and greatest civilization on earth across all time.

33
00:19:51,120 --> 00:20:27,590
To get proof for his claim in six forty BC the Pharaoh took two newborn babies from poor families and handed them to a silent shepherd living in a lonely wilderness hut.

34
00:20:27,590 --> 00:21:04,900
As a hunt leader I know a child raised in silence learns no hunt or danger calls, so the Pharaoh silent shepherd trial bred only fear and confusion instead of speech.

35
00:21:04,900 --> 00:21:42,610
The strict royal order was to raise the babies on warm goat milk inside a lonely desert hill hut for two full years but never to utter even a single word in front of them.

36
00:21:42,610 --> 00:22:16,560
After two silent years passed one morning at sunrise the shepherd opened the door and both toddlers crawled forward clutching his knees while shouting bekos bekos loudly.

37
00:22:16,560 --> 00:22:52,550
The stunned shepherd rushed the news to the Egyptian royal palace and the Pharaoh ordered scholars and language experts from across the Mediterranean to appear urgently.

38
00:22:52,550 --> 00:23:30,420
After checking ancient manuscripts scholars declared bekos was not Egyptian at all but ancient Phrygian for bread and that verdict crushed the Pharaoh pride completely.

39
00:23:30,420 --> 00:24:04,770
In the thirteenth century Holy Roman Emperor Frederick the Second in medieval Sicily repeated the same test to see if isolated babies would speak Hebrew Greek or Latin.

40
00:24:04,770 --> 00:24:44,000
The emperor gave foster mothers strict royal orders in Sicily to feed and bathe the babies daily but never to cuddle them lovingly or sing soft songs and speak to them.

41
00:24:44,000 --> 00:25:20,230
The outcome was tragic and heartbreaking because without warm touch and loving voices every single infant failed to survive as chronicler Salimbene clearly recorded.

42
00:25:20,230 --> 00:25:56,940
When my Fatehpur scholars argued endlessly about the origin of speech in fifteen eighty, I kept babies in a silent palace to test if any tongue could bloom without teachers.

43
00:25:56,940 --> 00:26:34,530
In fourteen ninety three King James the Fourth of Scotland sent two newborn babies across a stormy sea to lonely Inchkeith island under the care of a deaf mute nurse.

44
00:26:34,530 --> 00:27:12,320
Years later the king proudly claimed that after reaching speaking age at five the isolated children had miraculously started speaking pure flawless Hebrew by themselves.

45
00:27:12,320 --> 00:27:49,350
Scottish King James hoped lonely island babies would speak Hebrew as God tongue, but scholars say the court mistook their unclear babble for holy speech and cheered.

46
00:27:49,350 --> 00:28:26,420
Modern historians dismiss this claim as religious pride because the Scottish king mistook the meaningless babble of toddlers for his favourite holy language of scriptures.

47
00:28:26,420 --> 00:29:06,850
The most reliable isolation test in India was conducted by Mughal Emperor Akbar the Great who built a grand separate silent palace near Fatehpur Sikri for the experiment.

48
00:29:06,850 --> 00:29:45,080
In fifteen seventy nine Akbar built a lonely guarded royal house called Gang Mahal at Fatehpur Sikri and placed thirty newborn infants there under strictly mute wet nurses.

49
00:29:45,080 --> 00:30:21,710
Four years later Akbar entered the palace and saw the young isolated children spoke no Arabic Persian or Sanskrit and only grunted like scared animals with hand gestures.

50
00:30:21,710 --> 00:31:00,580
In fifteen eighty three Akbar proved conclusively that human language is not a magical gift received from the sky but a fully social skill learned from family and society.

51
00:31:00,580 --> 00:31:39,050
Keeping thirty babies with mute nurses for four years in Gang Mahal, Akbar saw speech is no birth magic but a social skill learned from mother society and daily talk.

52
00:31:39,050 --> 00:32:16,280
The next burning question is exactly what modern biology says about when the ancient human body first gained the throat and brain structure needed for clear speech sounds.

53
00:32:16,280 --> 00:32:54,430
In nineteen eighty three scientists deep inside famous Israel Kebara Cave found an intact hyoid throat bone in a sixty thousand year old Neanderthal called Kebara Two.

54
00:32:54,430 --> 00:33:29,580
Modern lab tests showed that ancient bone matched the modern human throat bone exactly so the dumb caveman myth failed and Neanderthals likely spoke in full sentences.

55
00:33:29,580 --> 00:34:06,490
Beyond fossil bones nineteen nineties research on a large London family of fifty members helped geneticists identify the FOXP Two language gene located on chromosome seven.

56
00:34:06,490 --> 00:34:41,480
Without this vital FOXP2 gene on chromosome seven, the growing human brain cannot control rapid tongue and lip movements or understand the rules of complex grammar at all.

57
00:34:41,480 --> 00:35:20,230
When scientists read Neanderthal DNA, they found the exact same FOXP2 variant, which means the shared blueprint for speech was fully ready four hundred thousand years ago.

58
00:35:20,230 --> 00:35:54,700
Wild chimpanzees in dense forests keep their voice box high in the throat, so they can easily swallow fresh water and breathe at the same time without ever choking to death.

59
00:35:54,700 --> 00:36:30,570
In humans the voice box dropped much lower over two million years to create clear tones and words, so we can speak beautifully but face a serious danger of choking on food.

60
00:36:30,570 --> 00:37:08,520
Holding the sixty thousand year old hyoid bone from Kebara Cave, I realized this tiny throat bone proves ancient humans were never voiceless brutes stumbling in silence.

61
00:37:08,520 --> 00:37:44,870
Evolution over millions of years accepted this deadly choking risk only because the power of language for survival proved far greater than any danger it created for humans.

62
00:37:44,870 --> 00:38:19,820
As an archaeologist I say a fault in the FOXP Two gene on chromosome seven breaks tongue lip control in young human kids, proving speech power hides deep in brain biology.

63
00:38:19,820 --> 00:38:55,810
To imagine the sound of the first language on ancient African soil, researchers now study the lifestyle of the remote old San people living in the vast Kalahari desert.

64
00:38:55,810 --> 00:39:31,840
Their Khoisan language uses sharp suction sounds made by pressing the tongue against the palate to create strange rhythmic clicks that sound completely unique during hunts.

65
00:39:31,840 --> 00:40:09,070
More than fifty linguists believe human first speech was no sweet song but a clever mix of sharp clicks and whistles used for silent contact during dangerous night hunting.

66
00:40:09,070 --> 00:40:46,420
In 2011 British scientist Quentin Atkinson published a groundbreaking study in the famous Science journal after analyzing the basic sounds of 504 languages worldwide.

67
00:40:46,420 --> 00:41:22,490
He found that ancient click languages in southern Africa hold more than one hundred separate basic sounds, far richer than languages in any other region across the globe.

68
00:41:22,490 --> 00:41:56,960
Today seven thousand tongues survive on Earth, and matching sound stocks of each language shows the great journey of speech worldwide began from ancient African click tunes.

69
00:41:56,960 --> 00:42:33,630
But as humans moved farther from Africa, sound counts kept falling, with only twenty five left in remote South America and just thirteen surviving in Hawaiian speech.

70
00:42:33,630 --> 00:43:13,580
Around seventy thousand years ago during a harsh ice age, a brave band of humans ignored frost and storms to cross the narrow Red Sea at Bab el Mandeb and spread worldwide.

71
00:43:13,580 --> 00:43:53,450
Some reached India along the coast, some reached China, some reached Europe and some crossed Siberia into America, so long isolation split speech into countless words.

72
00:43:53,450 --> 00:44:30,960
Thus thousands of major families like Indo European, Dravidian and Sino Tibetan were born, proving how vast geographic spread shaped the creative power of human society.

73
00:44:30,960 --> 00:45:05,750
Modern thinker Michael Corballis argues in two thousand two that human first language did not begin with the mouth but started its long journey through skilled hand gestures.

74
00:45:05,750 --> 00:45:44,300
Wild apes cannot share new daily facts through screams but can craft fine signals with hands, so early humans first exchanged ideas through full face and hand gestures.

75
00:45:44,300 --> 00:46:19,330
But hand signs vanish in dark nights and fail when hands hold sharp hunting spears, so humans slowly turned those silent gestures into warm clear sounds of the throat.

76
00:46:19,330 --> 00:46:59,320
Humans own neither sharp lion teeth nor swift cheetah speed in the wild, so a lone person wandering in the vast African savanna was merely helpless and easy prey for lions.

77
00:46:59,320 --> 00:47:37,470
But through language alone, millions of weak humans united to raise vast armies, build pyramids in Egypt, write constitutions and send rockets to the surface of the moon.

78
00:47:37,470 --> 00:48:13,940
Speech is far more than simple talk, for it works like an unseen global web that tightly ties billions of human minds together into one network across seven continents.

79
00:48:13,940 --> 00:48:52,890
So whether it was Pharaoh Bekos in Egypt or vast desert clicks, human first language was never one magic word but a strong urge to join each other across deep darkness.

80
00:48:52,890 --> 00:49:28,400
Which sweet word in the mother language you speak every day at home feels most precious to your heart, so please write it down for us in the comment box below tonight.

81
00:49:28,400 --> 00:50:07,790
Greek Herodotus notes Psamtik Bekos trial, Abu Fazl notes Akbar silent palace, texts note James trial, and modern linguistics rejects any single magical mother tongue.

82
00:50:07,790 --> 00:50:46,260
Comment your most beloved word from your sweet mother tongue below tonight, and let us see how many world languages gather like a grand village fair under this long video.

83
00:50:46,260 --> 00:51:23,130
In our next episode we enter ancient Nalanda great library that once held nine million books, where huge flames reportedly burned without pause for three whole months.

84
00:51:23,130 --> 00:51:58,480
